📅 Day 2 – Measures of Central Tendency
Measures of Central Tendency tell us the central or average value of a dataset.
There are three main measures:
Mean
Median
Mode
1. Mean (Average)
Definition
The sum of all values divided by the total number of values.
Formula
Mean = ΣX ÷ N
Where:
ΣX = Sum of all values
N = Number of observations
Example
Data: 10, 20, 30, 40, 50
Mean = (10 + 20 + 30 + 40 + 50) ÷ 5
Mean = 30
Advantages
Easy to calculate.
Uses all observations.
Disadvantages
Affected by outliers.
2. Median
Definition
The middle value after arranging data in ascending order.
Example (Odd)
5, 8, 10, 12, 20
Median = 10
Example (Even)
5, 8, 10, 12
Median = (8 + 10) ÷ 2
Median = 9
Advantages
Not affected by outliers.
Good for skewed data.
3. Mode
Definition
The value that appears most frequently.
Example
2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6
Mode = 4
Uses
Finding the most common item.
Useful for categorical data.
Comparison
Measure
Meaning
Best Used When
Mean
Average
No outliers
Median
Middle value
Data has outliers
Mode
Most frequent value
Most common category/value
Effect of Outliers
Data A
10, 12, 14, 16, 18
Mean = 14
Median = 14
Data B
10, 12, 14, 16, 100
Mean = 30.4
Median = 14
Observation
The Mean changes significantly because of the outlier (100), while the Median remains almost unchanged.
Real-Life Applications
Mean
Average salary
Average marks
Monthly sales
Median
House prices
Income distribution
Property values
Mode
Most sold product
Most common clothing size
Most common blood group
⭐ Key Takeaways
Statistics helps us collect, organize, analyze, interpret, and present data.
Data can be Qualitative or Quantitative.
Population is the entire group; Sample is a subset of that group.
Variables are characteristics that can take different values.
Scales of Measurement are Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, and Ratio.
Mean = Average.
Median = Middle value.
Mode = Most frequent value.
Median is preferred when the dataset contains outliers.
